Galactic Empire Planet Configuration Table
Version ge-next 0.4

LEVEL4 {}

Configuration for the neutral zone and faction outposts.
Names and outpost roles are synchronized to saved faction planets at startup
and on planetary updates. Descriptions come from the loaded MSG definitions.
Object kinds, coordinates, environment and resources are creation-only.
Changing object counts or order does not rebuild existing sectors.
Configuration synchronization does not reset stock, prices or refresh state.

Format PLANETS6: each section has a stable section tag, one to nine object
definitions, and an END marker. DONE terminates the file. Section tags identify
layouts, not their displayed planet names. Each of the six sections is required.

Each object has exactly twenty-five options, in this order:
TYP, NM, DSC, X, Y, ENV, RES, OUT, SHD, PHS, UPG, then R01 through R14.
Do not insert other options within an object.
Add or remove complete object blocks before the section END marker; there are
no DEF switches, explicit counts or reserved object slots. Keep option names
unique. The game reads section/type values and option order, not option names.
Keep PLTSTART first and do not change its name. Recreate MPOGEPLT.MCV after
editing and restart the module; adding objects does not require recompiling.

TYP: PLANET or WORMHOLE. END has no following object fields.
NM: displayed name, up to 19 characters; synchronized to saved faction planets.
Faction ownership comes from the section, not the displayed name.
Faction planets have no player user ID in their saved records.
DSC: faction planet description, up to 38 characters; blank for wormholes.
It replaces the owner line on planet scans and is read from this file at startup.
Description edits do not require recreating saved planets.
SHD/PHS: highest shield/phaser Mark sold here, 0-19; 0 disables that service.
These limits are read from the loaded definitions, not saved planet records.
Changing a limit requires reloading the MSG at module restart, not a DB reset.
They apply independently of the ship class equipment limits and faction standing.
Use 0 for wormholes. Trade-in credit, fees and global equipment prices are unchanged.
UPG: offered upgrades, using the same bit values as ship loadouts. Add the
values for each offered upgrade; 0 = none, 255 = all eight upgrades.
1 = enhanced scanners, 2 = armor, 4 = acceleration boost, 8 = scanner boost,
16 = enhanced lock, 32 = damage control, 64 = transponder control,
128 = neutron core. Ship compatibility and player unlocks still apply.
Any planet offering transponder control can install it or adjust its mode.
UPG is loaded from this file at restart, not saved in planet records.
Wormholes use 0. Multiple planets may offer the same upgrades.
R01-R14: preferred stock, 0-2000000000. Zero gradually drains stock to zero.
A zero target never generates new stock; an empty zero-target item stays empty.
01 men, 02 missiles, 03 torpedoes, 04 ion cannons, 05 flux pods, 06 food,
07 fighters, 08 decoys, 09 troops, 10 zippers, 11 jammers, 12 mines,
13 gold, 14 spies. These values are in item units, not tons.
Each planetary pass moves finite, offered stock partway toward its preferred
level with random variation. Stock near preferred continues to fluctuate.
Unlimited items and items not offered for sale ignore their preferred values.
New planets start offered finite stock at preferred levels, then immediately
run one randomized restock pass. Unlimited and unsold stock are not reseeded.
Changing preferred values requires a module restart, not a database reset.
Use zero for all wormhole targets. Preferred values are not storage caps.
X/Y: creation coordinates, 100 through 9900 (5000 is the sector center).
ENV/RES: creation values: 0 = Poor, 1 = Marginal, 2 = Good, 3 = Very good.
OUT: 0 = None, 1 = Zygor, 2 = Tahanian, 3 = Enforcer, 4 = Lydoria,
5 = Sarten, 6 = Murdonia, 7 = Vakory, 8 = Zygor Express, 9 = Trans-Gal HQ, 10 = Sarten shipyard. Wormholes use 0.
The outpost role is synchronized; the selected refresh routine manages live stock.

Sector 0 0 object 1 must be the Zygor planet (role 1). Each other section
must start with its primary outpost planet and matching role. Objects receive
consecutive numbers in file order. Express uses the same layout at all four
locations and is not created when UNIVMAX is below 50.

